\ul{
\li{Giving a lattice and #{\II}-algebra structure to #{n}-simplices.
(And also simplifying the lattice structure on the
[polynomial lattice](stt-009W))}
\li{Defining [slice algebras](stt-00UQ) and [opposite algebras](stt-00TG)}
\li{The definition of [conjunctive](stt-00TW) and [disjunctive](stt-00DY) types}
  \li{Showing that, assuming that [cubes separate](stt-00J7),
  the global points of #{\Delta^1} are the booleans, and the [directedness of the interval](stt-00VP), then the interval has
  the ['conjunctive' property](Synthetic.Conjunctive).}
}

\p{One nice observation that was made in the workshop was that we can take advantage
of the module system, and the fact that our interval is a 'regular' type (not built-in),
to formalise dual concepts by instantiating modules with the opposite lattice. This
is particularly useful because we don't necessarily assume that we have opposite
categories. This can be seen in the module \ref{Synthetic.Conjunctive} which simply
reexports \ref{Synthetic.Disjunctive}. Currently, whilst our definition
of [lattice](stt-009T) is defintionaly involutive, it fails to satisfy the law
#{i \le_{A} j = j \le_{A^{op}} i} \em{by definition}.
This is because #{\le} is defined in terms of #{\land} as opposed to being a part
of the data. This lack of definitional equality might make the strategy of
instantiating modules with the opposite lattice have some quality of life issues
down the line. This can be fixed by adding #{\le} as part of the data such that
it satisfies #{i \le j \iff i \land j = i} or alternatively, but maybe slightly
more hackily, we may define #{i \le j := (i \land j = i) \times (i \lor j = j)}.}


\p{Since the workshop [Evan](ecavallo) has continued to work on lattice algebra,
working towards the fact that simplices are affine (arise as the spectrum of
an #{\II}-algebra, and using this in combination with the quasicoherence principle
to show that they are categories.)
}

